Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-25-2001||03:35 PM||||Thinking of building/buying a hummer next summer after we get moved..<P>checking out the prices of the kits from Urban Gorilla, a kit would cost me about $10,000 for the basic package, then by the time we add all the extras on,  is it worth it?<P>When we can get a USED one from Lynch hummer for $36.000 ? (pro: its actually a hummer and not a kit.. con: price of parts compared to chevy surburban parts)<P>Anyone build one of these yet and how did it work out?<P>info pages:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.urbgorilla.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.urbgorilla.com/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.lynchhummer.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lynchhummer.com/</A> <P>Project after that one?  Lambo kit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||06-25-2001||03:44 PM||||Itall sorta follows the normal kitcar idea. Im not too familiar with real Hummer, drivetrain etc. I think its probably a high cost maintainance vehicle. Just like me, you may end up with just as much money in it as buying the used one.....but a Suburban is a lot cheaper to keep running and repairing. If you really just want to impress people, get the real, but if you want to actually use it, and enjoy it, build it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||06-25-2001||03:56 PM||||GM is releasing the H2 Hummer in the next couple years or sooner.  It's going to be based on the full size truck platfrom.  Don't know what it will be priced, hopefully comparable to a suburban or tahoe.  Just want to toss up another alternative.<P>My choice would be to buy a used Hummer.  I was just looking at one that was in Desert Storm at a show this weekend.  The camo was changed to white and the interior had been redone and finished.  I should have asked questions.  Might be worth checking into ex-military Hummers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-25-2001||04:19 PM||||I thought about the ex military ones also.. that might be a good idea..  are they using all hummer parts or their own hybrid of a hummer?<P>And  on a real one you have all the options built in that would cost an arm to purchase seperatly (central air for your wheels,air, guages etc)||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||06-25-2001||04:50 PM||||Yeah, I was going say with the real one, you actually get all the gizmos, and you can actually USE it offroad.. I dunno if a suburban could climb 45 degree muddy banks..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||06-25-2001||05:06 PM||||I have had the curse, I mean honor, of working on 2 Hummers, a 93 and a 98<P> The thing is nothing is modular on the vehicle, as it was not intended to be simply worked on, but rather to have any and all parts survive any condition. For instance, replacing the starter involves romoving the belly pan, blast sheild, then removing the second first transmission crossmember.<P> The is also plenty of seondary electrical systems to work around, as well as the hydraulic system for the suspension<P>my bet - go with the chevy||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||06-25-2001||05:37 PM||||Ya, a guy around here bought a military surplus one for I think $5,000. painted green camo. Had to do lots of work, he uses it to go camping and hunting. Its always covered with 100 pnds of mud.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||06-25-2001||06:39 PM||||But hopefully it's not built like a Pontiac product and you won't have to get a new starter with every oil change?||||reg||1
